<h2>The Katiji philosophy</h2>
<p>In <em>Katiji</em>, the Supreme Court condensed the approach into propositions that courts quote to this day: the appellant gains nothing by delay; refusal to condone can defeat a meritorious matter at the threshold; "every day's delay must be explained" is a rule of pragmatism, not pedantry; and when substantial justice and technical considerations are pitted against each other, the cause of substantial justice deserves preference. The Court simultaneously warned that the approach is not a licence: the explanation must disclose bona fides, and delay born of negligence or strategy remains unprotected. Later case law has tightened the screws for chronic defaulters — particularly governmental litigants pleading only file movement — while preserving the liberal core for genuine cases.</p>

<h2>Building the application</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Compute first.</strong> Fix the date of decree, apply Section 12's copy-time exclusion using the certified-copy endorsements, and state the exact delay in days. An application that miscounts loses credibility at paragraph one.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Explain in segments.</strong> Break the delay period into phases — knowledge of the decree, steps taken, the supervening cause, resumption of diligence — and account for each with dates.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Document the cause.</strong> Medical records, the earlier proceeding's orders for Section 14 exclusion, counsel's advice where relied on, and the copy-application receipts. Averment without material is the commonest reason for rejection.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>File with the appeal.</strong> The condonation application accompanies the memorandum of appeal with a supporting affidavit; the respondent may oppose, and the court decides it before or with admission.</div>
</div>

<h2>Recognised causes and recognised failures</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Generally accepted:</strong> serious illness of the party; death in the family disrupting instructions; bona fide prosecution of a wrong remedy with due diligence; delay in obtaining certified copies; wrong but genuinely followed legal advice.</li>
  <li><strong>Generally rejected:</strong> unexplained gaps after the cause ended; "file was under process" without particulars; delay while the party speculated on the outcome of parallel proceedings; ignorance of the decree where summons and notices were duly served.</li>
  <li><strong>Costs as balance:</strong> courts frequently condone with costs, compensating the respondent for the disturbed repose without destroying the appellant's remedy.</li>
  <li><strong>Merits are relevant, cautiously:</strong> a strong prima facie appeal tilts discretion; but merits cannot substitute for an explanation.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The respondent's side of the motion</h2>
<p>Opposition is most effective when it is specific: confront the timeline with the record, show that the alleged cause ended long before filing, and invoke the respondent's accrued right — after limitation expires, the decree-holder acquires a valuable right that is not to be lightly disturbed. Where condonation is nonetheless likely, press for terms: costs, deposit of the decretal amount in money decrees, and expedited hearing so that the appeal does not become a stay by another name.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Apply for the certified copy on the day of judgment. The copy-time exclusion under Section 12 is computed from the application to the delivery of the copy — the single cheapest way to buy lawful time, and the first thing an appellate court checks in the limitation calculation.</p>
</div>
